 A measurement older than `3 * interval + packets * timeout` is stale. When a second probe can run (`retry_loss_pct` or `dispersion_ms` is set, so `retry_packets` is in use) the window is `3 * interval + (packets + retry_packets) * timeout`. `min_replies` alone does not extend it. That same duration is the deadline for one probe round. The decision loop also wakes every `interval`, so a round that never finishes still withdraws once results are stale.
 
+The engine stores each prefix as its probes finish (#125). A round that hits the deadline keeps those finished results and leaves an unfinished prefix on its previous result, so staleness stays per result. The next pass continues with prefixes that are still due and does not wait out the interval of one that already finished. Due prefixes are ordered urgent first (a confirm streak, or a source that marked the target), then a shorter interval than `probe.interval` (VIP), then the engine interval (flow `top_n` and ordinary targets), then a longer interval (the flow tail). Prefixes in one tier keep the order the sources returned. The decision engine runs when the round stored at least one prefix, including a round the deadline cut short. It does not run when the deadline hits before any prefix finishes; those previous results stay, and the staleness check still withdraws them once they age out. Every packet still waits on `rate_limit_pps`.
+
 The retry sample replaces the first one. It runs when loss is at least `retry_loss_pct`, or when any host's RTT spread is over `dispersion_ms`. Too few replies do not start it. It runs once. Both waits go through `rate_limit_pps`.
 
 The engine remembers, per destination host, the prober that got a reply and starts the next round there (#120). The chain then moves forward: a later prober runs when the remembered one errors or gets no replies. Earlier probers run when the probe number of that host modulo `prober_recheck_rounds` is 1 (the 1st, 11th, 21st, … measurement at the default of 10), so a host that begins answering ICMP is noticed. Every provider shares that memory, and the rounds compare them with one probe type. When providers in one round first get replies from different probers, the later prober in the chain is remembered. A retry (loss or dispersion) uses the same start as the sample it rechecks and does not count as another probe toward the schedule. Every prober that runs still waits on `rate_limit_pps`; a prober that is skipped sends nothing. Hosts that are no longer in the target list are dropped on the next completed round, including a round that probes nothing because nothing is due. A prefix an improvement still keeps probed keeps its host's prober. Past `prober_memory`, the least recently probed hosts are dropped and their next measurement starts at the first prober. On-demand troubleshooting walks the full chain and does not read or update this memory. Nothing is announced.
